}}'''Munakata Taisha'' (Munakata Taisha) is a {{ill|lt=Shrine|Q845945|qid=Q845945|1=Shinto shrine}} located in {{ill|lt=Fukuoka Prefecture|Q123258|qid=Q123258|1=Fukuoka Prefecture}}{{ill|lt=Munakata City|Q819603|qid=Q819603|1=Munakata, Fukuoka}}. {{ill|UNKNOWN|qid=Q134917286|lt=Shikiuchi Shrine|1=Shikinaisha}} ({{ill|lt=Meishin Taisha|Q9610964|qid=Q9610964|1=Myōjin Taisha}}), one of {{ill|lt=Kami-gun|Q11590937|qid=Q11590937|lt=Yagami-gun|1=Yagami-gun}}. The former {{ill|lt=Shakaku|Q10444029|qid=Q10444029|1=Shakaku}} was {{ill|lt=Modern Shrine Ranking System #Government Shrine|Q712534|qid=Q712534|lt=Kanpei Taisha|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}, and currently it is {{ill|lt=Beppyo Shrine|Q10898274|qid=Q10898274|1=Beppyo shrine}} of {{ill|lt=Shinja Honcho|Q908077|qid=Q908077|1=Association of Shinto Shrines}}. There are over 7,000 shrines throughout Japan, including {{ill|lt=Munakata Shrine|Q11451876|qid=Q11451876|1=Munakata Shrines}}, {{ill|lt=Itsukushima Shrine|Q191763|qid=Q191763|1=Itsukushima Shrine}}, and {{ill|lt=Munakata Three Goddess It is the headquarters of the shrine that enshrines|Q10948069|qid=Q10948069|1=Three Munakata Goddesses}}, and in ``{{ill|lt=Nihon Shoki|Q813935|qid=Q813935|1=Nihon Shoki}}'', it is referred to as ``''Doshu Taka''''. {{ill|lt=Okinoshima|Q7082047|qid=Q7082047|1=Okinoshima (Fukuoka)}} floating in the {{ill|lt=Genkai Sea|Q498047|qid=Q498047|1=Genkai Sea}} is considered a sacred area, and many of the {{ill|lt=ancient|Q41493|qid=Q41493|1=Ancient history}}{{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=rituals|1=UNKNOWN}} offerings excavated on Okinoshima have been designated as {{ill|lt=national treasure|Q1139795|qid=Q1139795|1=National Treasure (Japan)}}. It is also called ``Ura Ise''.It was registered as a World Cultural Heritage Site in {{ill|lt=2017|Q25290|qid=Q25290|1=2017}} ({{ill|lt=Heisei|Q234510|qid=Q234510|1=Heisei era}}29) as one of the constituent assets of '''{{ill|lt="Island inhabited by the Gods" Munakata/Okinoshima and Related Heritage Group|Q324638|qid=Q324638|1=Sacred Island of Okinoshima and Associated Sites in the Munakata Region}}'''.

=== Overview ===
Munakata Taisha is a general term for three shrines: ``Okitsu-miya'' on {{ill|lt=Okinoshima|Q7082047|qid=Q7082047|1=Okinoshima (Fukuoka)}}, ``Nakatsu-miya'' on {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Chikuzen Oshima|1=UNKNOWN}}, and ``Hetsu-miya'' (Soja) on Tajima, {{ill|lt=Munakata City|Q819603|qid=Q819603|1=Munakata, Fukuoka}}, but nowadays it often refers to just ``Hetsu-miya''. Hetsu Shrine is enshrined in Tajima, Munakata City, and is also called ``Tajima-sama'' by the locals. There is also Okitsu-miya Yohaisho ('''Teitsu-miya'') on Chikuzen Oshima. On the map, Nakatsu-miya, which is 11 km away from Hetsu-miya, and Okitsu-miya, which is 49 km further away, are all in a straight line. It is one of the oldest shrines in Japan, whose history is recorded in the {{ill|lt=Kikiki|Q842308|qid=Q842308|1=Kiki (Shinto)}}, and has been a maritime route for politics, economy, and culture between the continent and the peninsula since ancient times. Since ancient times, he has been worshiped for his divine power as a {{ill|lt=god|Q178885|qid=Q178885|1=Deity}} of {{ill|lt=sea surface|Q125465|qid=Q125465|lt=sea|1=Sea level}} and {{ill|lt=traffic safety|Q1147899|qid=Q1147899|1=Road safety}}, but now he is worshiped not only on the sea but also on all roads, land, and traffic safety under the name of Doshu Taka.{{ill|lt=faith|Q5410500|qid=Q5410500|1=Faith}}. For this reason, many {{ill|lt=cars|Q1420|qid=Q1420|1=Car}} with Munakata Taisha Shrine {{ill|lt=stickers|Q2872553|qid=Q2872553|lt=stickers|1=Sticker}} can be seen in Fukuoka Prefecture and its surrounding areas, and there are also a large number of people who purchase a new car and receive a purification at the prayer hall. Additionally, the traffic safety charms that are worn on cars originate from Munakata Taisha Shrine.Okinoshima Island, where Okitsu-miya is located, is located at a key point for maritime traffic.Since ancient times, people have been called ``Oiwasama'' because people were not allowed to enter the island and reveal what they saw or heard, and the entire island is {{ill|lt=go|Q11488760|qid=Q11488760|1=御}}{{ill|lt=divine body|Q327532|qid=Q327532|1=Shintai}}. For this reason, even today {{ill|lt=women are prohibited|Q11446290|qid=Q11446290|1=Nyonin Kinsei}}, and even men must undergo {{ill|lt=misogi|Q1936265|qid=Q1936265|1=Misogi}} before landing. This is sometimes said to be gender discrimination, but there is a theory that the island's god is a female god ({{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Tashin Himekami|1=UNKNOWN}}) and that women who land on the island are said to be jealous and have a curse, but this is not confirmed. Excavations on Okinoshima Island have been carried out for more than ten years since 1952, and a large amount of ritual artifacts (offerings) such as ancient ritual remains and ornaments from the 4th to 9th centuries were discovered, as well as stone tools, pottery, and other artifacts from the {{ill|lt=Jomon period|Q52824|qid=Q52824|1=Jōmon period}} to the {{ill|lt=Yayoi period|Q207858|qid=Q207858|1=Yayoi period}}. For this reason, Okinoshima is commonly called the ``{{ill|lt=Shosoin|Q2379007|qid=Q2379007|1=Shōsōin}}'' of the sea, and it is remembered that it has been the subject of {{ill|lt=faith|Q5410500|qid=Q5410500|1=Faith}} by {{ill|lt=Amajin|Q9495537|qid=Q9495537|1=Ama People}} and others since prehistoric times. Currently, it is designated as an emergency evacuation port during typhoons. The plaque of {{ill|lt=Divine Rescript|Q11588781|qid=Q11588781|1=Commandment}} that is hung in the shrine's worship hall is {{ill|lt=Calligraphy|Q11498466|qid=Q11498466|1=Calligraphy}} by {{ill|lt=Prince Fushiminomiya Sadaai|Q466326|qid=Q466326|1=Prince Fushimi Sadanaru}}, and the plaque at Munakata Palace is the script of Imperial Envoy Shonii Gon Dainagon, Akitaka Hamuro.{{ill|lt=Egypt|Q79|qid=Q79|1=Egypt}}{{ill|lt=Archaeologist|Q3621491|qid=Q3621491|1=Archaeologist}} {{ill|lt=Sakuji Yoshimura|Q2740406|qid=Q2740406|1=Sakuji Yoshimura}} advocated A movement arose to designate the ritual sites of Noshima and the Munakata region as {{ill|lt=World Heritage|Q9259|qid=Q9259|1=World Heritage Site}}, and in 2009, "Okitsu-miya, Nakatsu-miya, Hetsu-miya" and "Okitsu-miya Yohaisho" were established. It was added to the tentative list as a group of related heritage sites including the ``Island inhabited by the Gods,'' Munakata/Okinoshima, and was nominated in 2015, and in July 2017, it was officially registered as a World Heritage Site at the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=UNESCO|1=UNKNOWN}} World Heritage Committee meeting in {{ill|lt=Poland|Q36|qid=Q36|1=Poland}} and {{ill|lt=Krakow|Q31487|qid=Q31487|1=Kraków}}.

=== Enshrined God ===
Each of the three shrines enshrines the following gods, collectively known as {{ill|lt=Three Goddesses of Munakata|Q10948069|qid=Q10948069|1=Three Munakata Goddesses}} (Munakata Okami).
* Okitsuguu Shrine: {{ill|lt=Takiribime|Q10932870|qid=Q10932870|lt=Tashin Himekami|1=Tagorihime}}
* Nakatsugu Shrine: {{ill|lt=Tagitsuhime|Q10932592|qid=Q10932592|lt=Tanatsu Himekami|1=Tagitsuhime}}
* Hetsu Shrine: {{ill|lt=Ichikishima Hime|Q11059454|qid=Q11059454|lt=Ichikishima Himekami|1=Ichikishima-hime}}

