=== Munakata Taisha Hetsumiya ===
* Main shrine (first shrine, Ichikishima Hime is the enshrined deity. It was built by {{ill|lt=Ujisada Munakata|Q10204112|qid=Q10204112|1=Munakata Ujisada}} and is said to have been based on the main shrine of Nakatsu-miya on Oshima. Important cultural property)
* Hall of worship (gabled roof, with a plaque from Munakata Palace. Built by {{ill|lt=Takakage Kobayakawa|Q1143508|qid=Q1143508|1=Kobayakawa Takakage}}. Important cultural property)
* Second Shrine (Okitsu-miya branch shrine, enshrined as Tagori-hime)
* Third shrine (Nakatsumiya branch shrine, Tagitsuhime is the enshrined deity)
* Takamiya Festival Hall (The ancient ritual remains at Byetsu-miya Shrine and the Shimo-Takamiya ritual site have been protected and newly constructed directly above the site. Today, large festivals in spring and autumn, as well as rituals in the garden are still held there.)
* Prayer Hall (Newly built in the Heisei period. Sakaki trees are planted in the center, and the garden is designed to look like Okinoshima Island. Various prayers are accepted at the counter.)
* Ceremonial Hall (A hall dedicated to events such as weddings. The one that was previously located at Yasukuni Shrine in Tokyo was relocated.)
* Seimei-den (a hall where the shrine maidens of Munakata Taisha perform dances etc.)
* Imperial Embassy (a palace where the Emperor, the Imperial Family, and their imperial envoys stay when they come to the shrine for offerings.)
* Shinmon (rebuilt in the Taisho period)
* Handshake (a newly installed roof or cover over the approach that connects the shrine to the shrine gate.Currently, the ``Shinmon'' is in front of the shrine, and both the shrine and the main shrine can only be seen from the side.)
* Haraisha (Haraito deity is enshrined in all directions)
* Chozuya (There is no {{ill|lt=ladle|Q11533616|qid=Q11533616|1=Hishaku}} and you receive water directly.)
* Otorii (donated by {{ill|lt=Takichi Aso|Q2743525|qid=Q2743525|1=Takichi Asō}})* Minor shrines (built in the early Edo period, a group of shrines in Munakata, Kamigun were donated by the then lord of Fukuoka, {{ill|lt=Mitsuyuki Kuroda|Q11678571|qid=Q11678571|1=Kuroda Mitsuyuki}}, and there are about 121 shrines around the outside of the main shrine.)
* Ebisusha
* Matsuosha
* Munakata Soreisha Shrine
* Munakata Gokoku Shrine
* Sacred Treasure Hall (Enjoy displaying and storing the national treasures listed below.)
* Munakata Chayu (rest facility with a cafe. Art works are also displayed and sold.)

=== Munakata Taisha Nakatsumiya ===
* Main shrine (built by Ujisada Munakata. Chigi on the roof shows the influence of {{ill|lt=Onmyo-do|Q1340540|qid=Q1340540|1=Onmyōdō}}. It is also said to be influenced by the ancient {{ill|lt=Yoshida Shinto|Q1760351|qid=Q1760351|1=Yoshida Shintō}} that viewed Munakata Shrine as a special shrine. There is also a theory that it has a longer history than the current Hetsu-miya main shrine and was modeled after it. It is a prefectural important monument.)
* Hall of worship (gabled roof. Rebuilt by the Kuroda clan, lord of Fukuoka.)
* Okumiya {{ill|lt=Mitake Shrine|Q11488964|qid=Q11488964|1=Mitake shrine}} (Ancient ritual remains at Nakatsunomiya, many magatama beads and iron swords were excavated. Rituals are still being held today.)
* Orijo-sha, Kengyusha (stone shrine-style shrines built across the Milky Way. Nakatsu-miya is also said to be the birthplace of the Japanese {{ill|lt=Tanabata|Q13423122|qid=Q13423122|1=Qixi Festival}} legend.)

=== Munakata Taisha Okitsu Shrine ===
* Shrine building (It is the smallest of the Sannomiya shrine buildings, and its roof is built into a huge rock.)
* Misogi place, Okitsu-miya shrine office, etc.

=== Okitsu Shrine Yohaisho ===
*Harukahaiden (A shrine building with a copper plate roof and gables that overlooks Okinoshima and Okitsu-miya shrines in the distance. Donated by the Kuroda clan, the lord of Fukuoka domain.)
* Torii, et al.

=== History ===In {{ill|lt=tradition|Q36192|qid=Q36192|1=Folklore}}, it has {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=origin|1=UNKNOWN}} in {{ill|lt=Japanese mythology|Q814158|qid=Q814158|1=Japanese mythology}}. {{ill|lt=Amaterasu Omikami|Q455602|qid=Q455602|1=Amaterasu}} and {{ill|lt=Susanoo|Q272993|qid=Q272993|lt=Amaterasu and Susa|1=Susanoo-no-Mikoto}} During Noo's Pledge|Q11284880|Pledge}} (Pledge), Amaterasu Omikami bit down the Sword of Suho, and the three goddesses of Munakata were born from the fragments that blew out. The origin of Munakata Taisha Shrine is said to be that the goddesses worshiped Amaterasu's ``''{{ill|lt=divine edict|Q11588781|qid=Q11588781|1=Commandment}}'''' and descended to the islands of Tsukushi Munakata in the Genkai Sea in the Kaihoku region to protect and help the imperial descendant {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Ninigi no Mikoto|1=UNKNOWN}}, and came to rule over this land. The only ``gods who descended from heaven to earth'' mentioned in {{ill|lt=Kiki|Q842308|qid=Q842308|1=Kiki (Shinto)}} are {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Ninigi no Mikoto|1=UNKNOWN}} and the three goddesses of Munakata who descended from heaven before {{ill|lt=Descent of Tenson|Q1084733|qid=Q1084733|1=Tenson kōrin}}. This is the oldest record of the Kiki in which the names of gods and their enshrined locations are clearly described. According to the {{ill|lt=Chikuzen Province|Q1047664|qid=Q1047664|1=Chikuzen Province}}{{ill|lt=Fudōki|Q1194534|qid=Q1194534|1=Fudoki}} regarding the shrine worship of Munakata Sangu Shrine, ``Saikaido Fudoki says, ``Since the time of Saimon Mountain, where Munakata Okami and his god were descended, , Ao-tama-Okikutsu-miya-no-omote, eight-shaku-purple-purple-oki-Okitsu-miya-no-omote, Yata-kagami-Okibetsu-miya-no-omote, and these three tables are placed in the form of the god's body. There is an anecdote that says, ``Sannomiya, Soku-no-in, Gin-gata-gun, Gonin-kai, descendants of Munakata Ki-Okai-no-Mikoto, and now Munakata Ason, etc.'' In ``{{ill|lt=Kojiki|Q813031|qid=Q813031|1=Kojiki}}'', Munakata is given the character ``Musakata,'' and is also written ``Munetakata'' or ``Munakata,'' but there is also a theory that it was originally Mizugata. It has been worshiped as the guardian deity of the local people since ancient times, and it is said that when {{ill|lt=Empress Jingu|Q232803|qid=Q232803|1=Empress Jingū}} prayed for safe voyages during her {{ill|lt=Sankan Conquest|Q11249435|qid=Q11249435|1=Three Han Campaign}}, she had a miraculous experience here, and it is said that she had a habit of sending envoys to Munakata whenever the situation arises. It was valued by the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Yamato Imperial Court|1=UNKNOWN}}, and since ancient times, whenever the capital was transferred, a spirit of our company was enshrined in the {{ill|lt=kendokoro|Q11635085|qid=Q11635085|1=Kashiko-dokoro}} (kashikodokoro) of the imperial court. This anecdote also reveals how he came to be worshiped as a {{ill|lt=guardian|Q131278957|qid=Q131278957|1=Guardian deities}} of safe navigation.With the introduction of {{ill|lt=ritsuryo system#Japanese ritsuryo system|Q742460|qid=Q742460|lt=ritsuryo system|1=Ritsuryō}}, {{ill|lt=kokugunri system|Q11421491|qid=Q11421491|lt=kokugun system|1=province-district-township system}}, {{ill|lt=Munakata county|Q6935606|qid=Q6935606|lt=one county of Munakata|1=Munakata District, Fukuoka}} was given as {{ill|lt=Temple territory|Q11458058|qid=Q11458058|lt=Shinryo|1=Jisha-ryō}}, The local {{ill|lt=gozoku|Q84066|qid=Q84066|1=Gōzoku}}{{ill|lt=Munakata clan|Q16481907|qid=Q16481907|lt=Munegata clan|1=Munakata clan}} served the shrine as {{ill|lt=Shinto priest|Q60523|qid=Q60523|lt=Shinto priest|1=Kannushi}}, and also took charge of the {{ill|lt=administration|Q31728|qid=Q31728|1=Public administration}} of {{ill|lt=Kamigun|Q11590937|qid=Q11590937|1=Kamigun}}. According to the {{ill|lt=stone monument|Q178743|qid=Q178743|1=Stele}} that records the origins of the Munakata clan, the head of the Munakata clan has welcomed the daughter of a Chinese {{ill|lt=merchant|Q215536|qid=Q215536|1=Merchant}} as his {{ill|lt=lawful wife|Q1765533|qid=Q1765533|1=Seishitsu}} for two generations. Also, {{ill|lt=Munegata Tokuzen|Q11451927|qid=Q11451927|1=Munakata no Tokuzen}} put his daughter {{ill|lt=Amago Musume|Q11464914|qid=Q11464914|1=Amako no iratsume}} into the {{ill|lt=Inner Palace|Q5353683|qid=Q5353683|1=back palace}} of {{ill|lt=Emperor Tenmu|Q335181|qid=Q335181|1=Emperor Tenmu}}, and {{ill|lt=Hakuji|Q540118|qid=Q540118|1=Hakuchi (era)}} for 5 years ({ The first prince, {{ill|lt=Prince Takaichi|Q837590|qid=Q837590|1=Prince Takechi}}, who was born to the two in {{ill|lt=654|Q31109|qid=Q31109|1=654}}, achieved great success by helping his father in the {{ill|lt=Jinshin War|Q2378252|qid=Q2378252|1=Jinshin War}}, and was later appointed as {{ill|lt=Dajodaijin|Q1157586|qid=Q1157586|1=Daijō-daijin}}. {{ill|lt=King Nagaya|Q1963492|qid=Q1963492|1=Prince Nagaya}} was the son of Takaichi and also the ancestor of the {{ill|lt=Takashina clan|Q11673216|qid=Q11673216|1=Takashina clan}}. In {{ill|lt=807|Q30016|qid=Q30016|1=807}} ({{ill|lt=Daido (Japan)|Q1373191|qid=Q1373191|lt=Daido|1=Daidō}}2), 74 houses were donated, {{ill|lt=840|Q29936|qid=Q29936|1=840}} ({{ill|lt=Jowa (Japan)|Q1075605|qid=Q1075605|lt=Jōwa|1=Jōwa (Heian period)}} 7th year), he was conferred {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Junior Fifth Rank Lower|1=UNKNOWN}}, and then became {{ill|lt=Jōgan (Japan)|Q1075635|qid=Q1075635|lt=Jōgan|1=Jōgan}} first year) and {{ill|lt=Senior Second Rank|Q11123277|qid=Q11123277|1=Senior Second Rank}}. In the 4th year of {{ill|lt=Tenkei|Q1327340|qid=Q1327340|1=Tengyō}} (after the {{ill|lt=Tenkei War|Q11442602|qid=Q11442602|1=Tenkei War}}), advance further and obtain {{ill|lt=Shinkai|Q11591025|qid=Q11591025|1=Shinkai (divine rank)}} of {{ill|lt=Shoichii|Q11123258|qid=Q11123258|1=Senior First Rank}}. {{ill|lt=979|Q23784|qid=Q23784|1=979}} ({{ill|lt=Tengen (Japan)|Q1327101|qid=Q1327101|lt=Tengen|1=Tengen (era)}}2 years) The {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Grand Shrine|1=UNKNOWN}} position is established by {{ill|lt=Dajokan|Q1192939|qid=Q1192939|1=Daijō-kan}}.After the {{ill|lt=Kamakura period|Q236205|qid=Q236205|1=Kamakura period}}, the {{ill|lt=Munakata clan|Q16481907|qid=Q16481907|1=Munakata clan}}, who was the descendant of the Munakata clan and inherited the position of the Daiguji family, became samurai and grew into a powerful kokujin feudal lord, but during the Sengoku period, {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Ouchi clan|1=UNKNOWN}}, {{ill|lt=Otomo clan|Q1096662|qid=Q1096662|1=Ōtomo clan}}, {{ill|lt=Shoni clan|Q2975368|qid=Q2975368|1=Shōni clan}}, and other nearby feudal lords were mobilized for wars, and Munakata Taisha Shrine also became the target of military attacks and was frequently arsoned and destroyed, and the Munakata clan also declined. However, each time it was worshiped by the {{ill|lt=Imperial Court (Japan)|Q30253177|qid=Q30253177|lt=Imperial Court|1=Imperial Court of Japan}} and {{ill|lt=Samurai|Q2927809|qid=Q2927809|1=Buke}} and was repeatedly rebuilt. The current Hetsu Shrine {{ill|lt=main hall|Q2044994|qid=Q2044994|1=Honden}}, with its beautiful large shingled roof, was rebuilt by the chief priest {{ill|lt=Ujisada Munakata|Q10204112|qid=Q10204112|1=Munakata Ujisada}} in the 6th year of Tensho ({{ill|lt=1578|Q6707|qid=Q6707|1=1578}}). Ken, Hetsu-miya {{ill|lt=haiden|Q3125745|qid=Q3125745|1=Haiden (Shinto)}} was rebuilt in Tensho 16 ({{ill|lt=1590|Q6764|qid=Q6764|1=1590}}) by {{ill|lt=Takakage Kobayakawa|Q1143508|qid=Q1143508|1=Kobayakawa Takakage}}, the lord of Chikuzen. Both the main hall of worship of Hetsu Shrine are designated as national {{ill|lt=Important Cultural Properties|Q1188622|qid=Q1188622|1=Important Cultural Property (Japan)}}. The rituals carried out by the Munakata clan were then passed on to the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Kusakari clan|1=UNKNOWN}} ({{ill|lt=Shigetsugu Kusakari|Q22126274|qid=Q22126274|1=Shigetsugu Kusakari}}). During the Edo period, it is often reported that the {{ill|lt=Chikuzen|Q242409|qid=Q242409|1=Chikuzen}}{{ill|lt=Fukuoka Domain|Q208766|qid=Q208766|1=Fukuoka Domain}} lord {{ill|lt=Kuroda clan|Q2975305|qid=Q2975305|1=Kuroda clan}} constructed and repaired shrine buildings, and {{ill|lt=donated|Q11455717|qid=Q11455717|1=contribution}} the shrine's territory. After that, from the end of the Edo period to the Meiji period ({{ill|lt=Haibutsu Kishaku|Q702298|qid=Q702298|1=Haibutsu kishaku}}, Byobuyama {{ill|lt=Chinkokuji|Q11651157|qid=Q11651157|1=Chinkoku-ji}}, which was {{ill|lt=Jinguji|Q1074971|qid=Q1074971|1=Jingū-ji}}, was separated from the Taisha shrine.Also, in {{ill|lt=1871|Q7746|qid=Q7746|1=1871}} ({{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|qid=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}}4), it was listed as ``Munakata Shrine'' in {{ill|UNKNOWN|qid=Q134917280|lt=Kokuheichusha|1=Kokuhei Chūsha}} in the {{ill|lt=Modern Shrine Ranking System|Q712534|qid=Q712534|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}. On April 22 of the following year, he was promoted to {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Kanpei Chusha|1=UNKNOWN}}. Then, on July 11, {{ill|lt=1901|Q2035|qid=Q2035|1=1901}} ({{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|qid=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}}34), it was promoted to the highest rank, {{ill|lt=Modern Shrine Ranking System#Kansha|Q712534|qid=Q712534|lt=Kanpei Taisha|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}.

After {{ill|lt=Showa|Q327567|qid=Q327567|1=Shōwa era}} {{ill|lt=World War II|Q362|qid=Q362|1=World War II}}, the shrine grounds were in ruins. Born in Akama (Akama district, Munakata city), he has worshiped Munakata Taisha Shrine since childhood. It was built with a donation from {{ill|lt=Sazo Idemitsu|Q7429431|qid=Q7429431|1=Sazō Idemitsu}}, a businessman whom he admired, and he worked hard to encourage the government to investigate and excavate the Okinoshima ritual ruins where Okitsu Shrine is located.

=== Kei Yukiyuki ===
{{ill|lt=1983|Q2434|qid=Q2434|1=1983}} ({{ill|lt=Showa|Q327567|qid=Q327567|1=Shōwa era}}58), Crown Prince Akihito (later {{ill|lt=Emperor Empress Akihito|UNKNOWN|lt=Retired Emperor Akihito}}) and his wife Michiko (later {{ill|lt=Emperor Empress Michiko|Q87610|qid=Q87610|1=Empress Michiko}}) visited Hetsu Shrine.

{{ill|lt=2013|Q1998|qid=Q1998|1=2013}} ({{ill|lt=Heisei|Q234510|qid=Q234510|1=Heisei era}}25), {{ill|lt=Naruhito|Q217096|qid=Q217096|lt=Crown Prince Naruhito|1=Naruhito}} visited Hetsu Shrine.

{{ill|lt=2017|Q25290|qid=Q25290|1=2017}} (2017) {{ill|lt=October 29|Q2951|qid=Q2951|1=October 29}}, Emperor Akihito and Empress Michiko became the first emperors and empresses in history to visit Hetsu Shrine to pay their respects.<ref>{{Cite news|Japanese book|title=Fukuoka) Their Majesties visit Munakata Taisha, a World Heritage Site: Asahi Shimbun Digital|url=https://www.asahi.com/sp/articles/ASKBY5R38KBYTIPE012.html|accessdate=2018-05-08|language=ja-JP|work=Asahi Shimbun Digital}}{{Broken link|date=March 2024}}</ref>.

In addition, as a member of the imperial family, {{ill|lt=Emperor Showa|Q34479|qid=Q34479|1=Hirohito}}'s younger brother {{ill|lt=archaeologist|Q3621491|qid=Q3621491|1=Archaeologist}} {{ill|lt=Prince Takahito Mikasa|Q280229|qid=Q280229|1=Takahito, Prince Mikasa}} visited the shrine many times, and he also composed waka poems about Okinoshima.

=== Sessha ===
* Kamiminato Munakata Shrine (Old Shrine) - Outside the precincts of the shrine (Autumn Grand Festival Tajima Hojokai, {{ill|lt=Miare Festival|Q11278738|qid=Q11278738|1=Miare Festival}}, where mikoshi of the three goddesses are lined up and dedicated)

=== Shinto rituals and festivals ===
* New Year's Festival - Shinto rituals to celebrate the New Year, such as Saidan Festival. 1st to 3rd of New Year every year.
* Spring Festival - April 1st-3rd every year.
* Summer Purification - Held at the end of July every year.
* {{ill|lt=Tanabata|Q13423122|qid=Q13423122|1=Qixi Festival}} Festival - Held every year on {{ill|lt=lunar calendar|Q11511066|qid=Q11511066|1=Japanese Lunisolar Calendar}} at the evening of July 7th at the Nakatsu Shrine of {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Chikuzen Oshima|1=UNKNOWN}}. There are {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Kengyu|1=UNKNOWN}}sha and {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Orijo|1=UNKNOWN}}sha, in front of which bamboo bamboo leaves with strips of paper are erected to pray for improvement in the craft. A ritual is held to predict the fate of men and women by looking at their reflections in the water, and a Tanabata {{ill|lt=calligraphy|Q11498466|qid=Q11498466|1=calligraphy}} competition is also held. According to the Shohei Annual Events, it is {{ill|lt=1346|Q6080|qid=Q6080|1=1346}} ``Tanabata Mushifuri Shinto ritual on the seventh day of July''.
* Autumn Festival - October 1st to 3rd every year. Prior to the event at Hetsu Shrine's main shrine on the 1st to 3rd, there is a {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=divine welcome|1=UNKNOWN}} ritual held at Nakatsu-miya and Okitsu-miya shrines, and a {{ill|lt=divine celebration|UNKNOWN|lt=marine divine celebration}} is held with colorful flags and banners set up by fishing boats (Miare Festival). At the main shrine, {{ill|lt=Okina|Q11610439|qid=Q11610439|1=okina}} dances, customary dances, {{ill|lt=yabusame|Q1886157|qid=Q1886157|1=Yabusame}}, dedication {{ill|lt=sumo|Q40561|qid=Q40561|1=Sumo}}, etc. are performed. Its origins date back to the {{ill|lt=Heian period|Q193292|qid=Q193292|1=Heian period}}, and its official name is ``Tajima Hojokai.''
* Kencha Festival - Held on October 17th every year.
* Harvest Festival - A new festival that began to be held every year after the Heisei Emperor and Empress paid their respects to our company.* West Japan Chrysanthemum Exhibition - A chrysanthemum exhibition held every November. A large number of cultivated chrysanthemums from Kyushu and other prefectures in western Japan will be on display and appreciated.
* Ancient Ceremony Festival, Hibisai Festival - A secret winter festival with a history of over 800 years that is held in mid-December every year.
* New Year's Purification - New Year's Eve Shinto ritual held on New Year's Eve every year.

=== Cultural assets ===

=== National Treasure ===
* '''Items excavated from the Okitsu-miya ritual site of Munakata Taisha Shrine in Fukuoka Prefecture/Traditional Items excavated from the Oki-tsu-miya ritual site of Munakata Taisha Shrine in Fukuoka Prefecture'''' Bulk: A collection of artifacts excavated from over 20 ritual sites on Okinoshima, a remote island in the Genkai Sea. It was excavated during the first to third excavations from {{ill|lt=1954|Q18581|qid=Q18581|1=1954}} to {{ill|lt=1971|Q2475|qid=Q2475|1=1971}}. Spreads from {{ill|lt=Kofun period|Q459939|qid=Q459939|1=Kofun period}} to {{ill|lt=Heian period|Q193292|qid=Q193292|1=Heian period}} ({{ill|lt=4th century|Q8099|qid=Q8099|1=4th century}} to {{ill|lt=10th century|Q8052|qid=Q8052|1=10th century}}). The main artifacts excavated include various bronze mirrors, horse harnesses made of gilt bronze (gold plated on copper), including products from China and the Korean Peninsula, as well as Haji ware, Sansai pottery, talcum products, jade items, and swords. Among the excavated items are fragments of glass bowls thought to have been made by {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Persia|1=UNKNOWN}} and {{ill|lt=Sassanid|Q83891|qid=Q83891|1=Sasanian Empire}}. It is an extremely valuable academic resource that contributes to research in a variety of fields, including archaeology, art history, religious history, and ancient history. The results of three excavations have been published in the form of reports called ``Okinoshima,'' ``Zoku Okinoshima,'' and ``Munakata Okinoshima.'' These excavated items are on display at the sacred treasure museum of Munakata Taisha Shrine. In {{ill|lt=1962|Q2764|qid=Q2764|1=1962}}, the artifacts from the first and second excavations were designated as national treasures, and in {{ill|lt=2003|Q1986|qid=Q1986|1=2003}}, the artifacts from the third excavation were additionally designated as national treasures. It is the largest national treasure in Japan in terms of quantity, consisting of approximately 80,000 relics. <gallery perrow="3" widths="170px" heights="100px">File:Munakata-taisha ryutou.jpg|Gold bronze dragon head
File:Munakata-taisha takahata.jpg|Gold bronze high machine
File:Munakata-taisha sancai.jpg|Nara Sansai Small Jar
</gallery>

