=== Munakata Taisha and Sazo Idemitsu ==={{ill|lt=Idemitsu Kosan|Q2216770|qid=Q2216770|1=Idemitsu Kosan}}'s founder, {{ill|lt=Sazo Idemitsu|Q7429431|qid=Q7429431|1=Sazō Idemitsu}}, is from the former {{ill|lt=Munakata District|Q6935606|qid=Q6935606|1=Munakata District, Fukuoka}} Akama-juku, and has revered Munakata Taisha Shrine since childhood. In 1937, when he visited Betsu Shrine, he lamented the devastation of the shrine, and in 1942 he called for the formation of the ``Munakata Shrine Restoration Society'' and became its first president. After suspending its activities during and after the war, it was reorganized as the Munakata Taisha Reconstruction Society in 1969. The restoration of the main shrine and worship hall of Hetsu Shrine was largely due to Sazo's donations, but as he declined out of fear, there were no traces of his name within the shrine grounds for a long time, but there are now exhibits honoring Sazo at Shinpokan and other places. In the novel "{{ill|lt=A Man Called a Pirate|Q16046027|qid=Q16046027|1=A Man Called Pirate}}," which is based on Sazo, upon {{ill|lt=defeat|Q11499267|qid=Q11499267|1=defeat}}, a separate shrine was established within the company. }}It is depicted praying to the three goddesses of Munakata Shrine, who have been praying for the protection of {{ill|lt=Japan|Q17|qid=Q17|1=Japan}} and the {{ill|lt=Japanese people|Q16782073|qid=Q16782073|1=Ethnic Japanese}}. Sazo wrote in his book that he believed that Ise Shrine was the ancestor of the imperial family, and Munakata Taisha Shrine was the ancestor of the people. {{ill|lt=Tokyo|Q1490|qid=Q1490|1=Tokyo}}{{ill|lt=Idemitsu Art Museum|Q3076590|qid=Q3076590|1=Idemitsu Museum of Arts}} located in {{ill|lt=Chiyoda-ku|Q214051|qid=Q214051|1=Chiyoda, Tokyo}} is an art museum that exhibits artworks collected by Sazo.<ref>{{Cite web|Japanese book|url=https://idemitsu-museum.or.jp/about/outline/|title=About Idemitsu Museum of Art|accessdate=2024-09-24|website=Idemitsu Museum of Art website|publisher=Public The Idemitsu Museum of Art}}</ref> regularly holds special exhibitions such as the Munakata/Okinoshima Exhibition, and on October 17th every year, when the tea ceremony is held at Munakata Taisha Shrine, many of the famous tea utensils from the collection are put on display, and the {{ill|lt=Omotesenke|Q3278052|qid=Q3278052|1=Omotesenke}} master offers tea, commemorating the perpetuation of the shrine and remembering Sazo.

